=pod

=head1 NAME

changetrack-ng.conf - configuration file for changetrack-ng

=head1 SYNOPSIS

F</etc/changetrack-ng/changetrack-ng.conf>

=head1 DESCRIPTION

B<changetrack-ng> reads a two-section INI v2 config file on every run.
Lines beginning with C<#> are comments; blank lines are ignored.
Key-value pairs use C<key = value> syntax.

The config may contain notifier credentials (for example in C<otlp_headers>).
Set file permissions to C<0640> or stricter.

Scalar and array values support environment-variable expansion using
C<${VAR_NAME}>.  An undefined variable causes a startup parse error.

The file has two sections:

=over 4

=item B<[global]>

Runtime settings: VCS backend, notification channels, paths, etc.

=item B<[track]>

The list of files and directories to monitor.

=back

=head1 GLOBAL SETTINGS

=head2 Core

=over 4

=item B<vcs> = C<git> | C<rcs> | C<fossil> | C<svn> | C<hg>

VCS backend used to store change history.  Default: C<git>.

=item B<output_dir> = I<path>

Directory where VCS history is stored.  For whole-repo backends (git, fossil,
svn, hg) this is the root of a mirror tree.  For RCS, flat C<,v> files are
written here.  Default: C</var/lib/changetrack-ng>.

=item B<notify> = I<space-separated list>

Notification channels to enable.  Valid values: C<email>, C<webhook>,
C<otlp>, C<file>.  Multiple values are space-separated.
Leave empty to disable notifications.

=item B<push_remote> = I<remote ...>

For whole-repo VCS backends: space-separated list of Git/SVN/etc. remotes to
push to after each commit.  Leave empty to skip push.

=back

=head2 Email

=over 4

=item B<email_to> = I<address ...>

Global fallback email recipients, space-separated.  Used for tracked entries
that do not define specific per-file or per-directory recipient addresses
in the C<[track]> section.

=item B<email_from> = I<address>

Sender address for email notifications.  The token C<HOSTNAME> is replaced
with the system hostname at runtime.  Default: C<changetrack-ng@HOSTNAME>.

Note: Email notifications are naturally anti-spam; they have no heartbeat configuration and send zero emails during a clean no-change run.

=back

=head2 Webhook

=over 4

=item B<webhook_url> = I<url ...>

One or more URLs to HTTP POST change reports to.  Space-separated.
Required when C<notify> includes C<webhook>.

=item B<webhook_full_diff> = C<0> | C<1>

Include a full unified diff in the webhook payload.  Default: C<0> (summary only).

=item B<webhook_heartbeat> = C<0> | C<1>

POST a beacon even when there are no changes.  Default: C<0>.

=item B<webhook_insecure> = C<0> | C<1>

Disable TLS certificate verification for webhook delivery.  Only for
self-signed development setups.  Default: C<0>.

=back

=head2 OTLP / OpenTelemetry

=over 4

=item B<otlp_endpoint> = I<url>

Full OTLP/HTTP endpoint URL including the C</v1/logs> path.
The environment variables C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT> and
C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT> take precedence if set.
Required when C<notify> includes C<otlp>.

=item B<otlp_full_diff> = C<0> | C<1>

Include full diff in OTLP log records.  Default: C<0>.

=item B<otlp_compression> = C<gzip> | C<none>

HTTP body compression for OTLP exports.  Default: C<gzip>.

=item B<otlp_headers> = I<key=value ...>

Additional HTTP headers, space-separated.  Escape spaces within a value with
a backslash: C<Authorization=Bearer\ mytoken>.
Supports C<${VAR}> expansion, for example:
C<Authorization=Bearer\ ${OTLP_TOKEN}>.
For secret material, prefer C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS> when possible.

=item B<otlp_timeout> = I<seconds>

HTTP request timeout.  Default: C<10>.

=item B<otlp_insecure> = C<0> | C<1>

Disable TLS certificate verification.  Only for self-signed development
setups.  Default: C<0>.

=item B<otlp_resource_attributes> = I<key=value ...>

Additional OTLP resource attributes, space-separated.

=item B<otlp_heartbeat> = C<0> | C<1>

Send a heartbeat log record on every run, even when no changes are detected.
Default: C<1>.  Enables "alert on silence" monitoring patterns.

=back

=head2 File notifier

=over 4

=item B<notify_log_path> = I<path>

Append-only JSON log file path.  Required when C<notify> includes C<file>.
Default: C</var/log/changetrack-ng.log>.

=item B<file_full_diff> = C<0> | C<1>

Include full diff lines in file-notifier JSON records.  Default: C<1>.
Set to C<0> for metadata-only file notifier output.

=item B<file_heartbeat> = C<0> | C<1>

Append a heartbeat record on every run.  Default: C<0>.

=back

=head2 Ignore patterns

=over 4

=item B<ignore> = I<pattern ...>

Additional glob patterns to skip during directory traversal, space-separated.
Added to the built-in defaults, which already exclude: C<*.swp>, C<*~>,
C<#*#>, C<*.dpkg-*>, C<*.rpmnew>, C<*.pacnew>, C</etc/mtab>, C<ld.so.cache>,
and several other common noise files.

=back

=head2 Sensitive files

=over 4

=item B<sensitive_auto> = C<0> | C<1>

Auto-detect sensitive files by mode.  A file is treated as sensitive if it
has no group or world read bit (mode & 0044 == 0), e.g. mode C<0600>.
Default: C<1>.

=item B<sensitive_notify> = C<suppress> | C<allow>

How to handle sensitive-file diffs in outbound notifications.
C<suppress> replaces the diff with a placeholder; C<allow> sends the full
diff.  Default: C<suppress>.

Even when C<sensitive_notify = suppress>, notification payloads still include
the path of changed files.  This is an intentional auditability trade-off.

=back

=head2 Pre-commit checks

=over 4

=item B<warn_special_files> = C<0> | C<1>

Warn about special files (sockets, FIFOs, device nodes) and hardlinks.
Default: C<1>.

=back

=head2 Scheduling

=over 4

=item B<interval> = C<hourly> | C<daily> | I<cron-expression>

Informational: used by C<make install-systemd> and C<make install-cron>.
Does not affect runtime behavior.  Default: C<hourly>.

=back

=head2 Locking

=over 4

=item B<lock_path> = I<path>

Lock file path.  Default: C</var/run/changetrack-ng.lock>.

=item B<lock_timeout> = I<seconds>

Seconds to wait for the lock.  C<0> means skip silently if locked, which is
the cron-friendly default.  Default: C<0>.

=back

=head1 TRACK SECTION

The C<[track]> section lists files and directories to monitor, one per line.

=head2 File entry

  /path/to/file [: email@example.com ...] [option=value ...]

=head2 Directory entry

  DIRECTORY /path/to/dir [: email@example.com ...] [option=value ...]

Directories are scanned recursively.

=head2 Per-entry options

=over 4

=item B<sensitive=auto|yes|no>

Override the global sensitive-file detection for this entry.

=back

=head2 Examples

  [track]
  /etc/passwd : root@localhost
  /etc/hosts
  DIRECTORY /etc/nginx : admin@example.com
  DIRECTORY /etc/ssl/private sensitive=yes

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

=over 4

=item C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT>

Overrides C<otlp_endpoint> when set.

=item C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT>

Overrides both C<OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT> and C<otlp_endpoint> for the
logs signal specifically (highest precedence).

=back

=head1 FILES

=over 4

=item F</etc/changetrack-ng/changetrack-ng.conf>

Default config location.

=item F</usr/local/etc/changetrack-ng/changetrack-ng.conf>

Installed default config when built with C<PREFIX=/usr/local>.

=back

